---@class Cis.callback.await
---@field since string 1.0.0
---@field realm "both"
---@field forwardsTo string AwaitCallback
---@param name string #a registered name
---@param ... ... #the arguments for the handler
---@return any #the handler's results, and on the server they survive a nil in the middle. A refusal RAISES rather than returning, so a caller that does not pcall takes the hit -- tryAwait is the non-raising form 
-- Why `any`: the handler's own result list, whose arity and types the handler alone decides -- the handler is a function in another resource, and its return contract is that resource's, not this library's
function Cis.callback.await(name, ...) end

---@class Cis.callback.awaitClient
---@field since string 1.0.0
---@field realm "server"
---@field forwardsTo string AwaitCallbackClient
---@param src integer #the target player
---@param name string #the callback the CLIENT answers
---@param ... ... #the arguments; the client caps them at 32
---@return boolean, any #true plus the client's results; false plus a reason for a bad target, a disconnect, a timeout or a drop 
-- Why `any`: the handler's own result list, whose arity and types the handler alone decides -- the handler is a function in another resource, and its return contract is that resource's, not this library's
function Cis.callback.awaitClient(src, name, ...) end

---@class Cis.callback.call
---@field since string 1.0.0
---@field realm "both"
---@field forwardsTo string CallCallback
---@param name string #a registered name
---@param cb? function #the callback, invoked with the whole reply. It COSTS A COROUTINE PER CALL, which is what the caller was already paying for an await, and it is the only way to give a callback-style API to a consumer that cannot pass a function across
---@param ... ... #the arguments for the handler
---@return nil #nothing comes back: the answer only ever arrives as cb(ok, ...). On the client the pending key is discarded
function Cis.callback.call(name, cb, ...) end

---@class Cis.callback.callClient
---@field since string 1.0.0
---@field realm "server"
---@field forwardsTo string CallCallbackClient
---@param src integer #the target player
---@param name string #the callback the CLIENT answers
---@param cb? function #a consumer-side function, consumed on the consumer side, so it does not cross
---@param ... ... #the arguments; the client caps them at 32
---@return integer #the pending key. A bad target returns nothing and instead calls cb(false, reason)
function Cis.callback.callClient(src, name, cb, ...) end

---@class Cis.callback.register
---@field since string 1.0.0
---@field realm "both"
---@field forwardsTo string RegisterCallback
---@param name string #a non-empty handler name
---@param handler function|string #a function -- cis_libs itself only -- or a 'resource:Export' string, which is the form that works from a consumer
---@return boolean #true once bound. A refusal's reason goes to the log, NOT to the caller
function Cis.callback.register(name, handler) end

---@class Cis.callback.tryAwait
---@field since string 1.0.0
---@field realm "both"
---@field forwardsTo string TryAwaitCallback
---@param name string #a registered name
---@param ... ... #the arguments for the handler
---@return boolean, any #true plus the whole result list, so a nil in the middle survives; false plus a reason otherwise. Never raises 
-- Why `any`: the handler's own result list, whose arity and types the handler alone decides -- the handler is a function in another resource, and its return contract is that resource's, not this library's
function Cis.callback.tryAwait(name, ...) end

